(Bug #39 - a format string as the FIRST element of an inline collection
 made EVERY element of that collection print as a raw pointer. The
 integers were heap addresses: the first changed on every run of the
 same binary under ASLR while the second was stable rodata.

 This test is the register entry's full control table, because the
 diagnosis needs every row to be unambiguous:

   A vs B  - position was the tell. The format string FIRST broke the
             whole collection; second, everything rendered. A fix that
             only repairs position 1 has treated a symptom.
   C vs D  - the runtime tag was always right (C was correct before the
             fix); attaching a `treating` clause to the SAME named list
             broke it, which is what proves the defect was in STATIC
             element-type inference.
   E, F, G - always correct, kept as controls. They must pass on both
             sides of the fix; F in particular uses escaped braces and
             so contains no slot at all.)
a text called base is "core".
a list called src is ["{base}", "plain"].

Print "A literal 1st:".
print each item from ["{base}", "plain"].
Print "B literal 2nd:".
print each other from ["plain", "{base}"].
Print "C named plain:".
print each thing from src.
Print "D named treating:".
print each part from src treating "" as "EMPTY".
Print "E element 1:".
Print element 1 of src.
Print "F escaped only:".
print each lit from ["{{lit}}", "plain"].
Print "G no format:".
print each word from ["alpha", "beta"].
